  "output": "Reproduced. Here's the full picture:\n\n**Sample file** (`bug_report.csv`):\n```\nname,note\nBob, \"Hello, world\"\nAlice,fine\n```\n\n**Incorrect output:**\n```\n$ .venv/bin/python fmtcsv.py bug_report.csv\nTotal rows: 2\nTotal columns: 2\nFirst data row: ['Bob', ' \"Hello', ' world\"']\n```\n\nThe quoted field `\"Hello, world\"` is split into two fields — `' \"Hello'` and `' world\"'` — instead of staying intact. Note also the inconsistency: `Total columns` says 2 (counted from the header) while the first data row actually contains 3 fields.\n\n**Root cause — the A/B comparison** (run side by side):\n\n| Input | First data row | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Bob,\"Hello, world\"` (quote directly after comma) | `['Bob', 'Hello, world']` | ✅ correct |\n| `Bob, \"Hello, world\"` (space before the quote) | `['Bob', ' \"Hello', ' world\"']` | ❌ split |\n\nThe bug triggers when there's whitespace between the delimiter and the opening quote. Python's `csv` reader (following RFC 4180 strictly) only treats `\"` as a quoting character when it's the *first* character of the field; a leading space makes it a literal, so the comma inside becomes a real delimiter.\n\n**Why the naive fix is tricky:** enabling `skipinitialspace=True` would make Case B parse correctly, but it would also strip leading spaces from *unquoted* fields — which breaks the original requirement to preserve exact content (our test `test_quoted_commas_and_unquoted_spaces` expects `'  raw '` to stay verbatim). A proper fix needs to treat whitespace-then-quote as a quoted field while leaving unquoted field content untouched.\n\nRepro files kept in the workspace: `bug_report.csv` (breaking input) and `proper_quoted.csv` (working input). Want me to implement the fix — with a regression test for the space-before-quote case added to `test_fmtcsv.py`?",
